{
  "borough": "London Borough of Barking and Dagenham",
  "application_type": "Householder",
  "decision": "Refused",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed development, by reason of the design, scale and form of the rear dormer and two-storey outbuilding, would result in an overly dominant and visually discordant form of development that fails to respect the character of the host dwelling and surrounding area. The dormer, incorporating both a Juliet balcony and an inset terrace feature, would appear incongruous and uncharacteristic of rear roof extensions within the locality. Its recessed form, poor window alignment and atypical configuration would erode the visual consistency of the rear roofscape.&nbsp;Additionally, the two-storey outbuilding, incorporating a basement and measuring ~60.6m², would be excessive in scale for ancillary use and out of keeping with the character of the area and contrary to the built form of the Becontree Estate. The cumulative impact of these elements would harm the character and appearance of the host property and wider streetscene and as such would be contrary to:The National Planning Policy Framework (MHCLG, December 2024)Policies D4 and HC1 of the London Plan (March 2021)Policies SP2, SPP6, DMD1 and DMD4 of the LBBD Local Plan (September 2024)The Residential Extensions and Alterations SPD (February 2012)"
  ],
